        <section id="run" aria-labelledby="run-title">
          <h1 class="page-title" id="run-title">Running outside a browser</h1>
          <p class="intro">Read this when you want a site in your terminal, in CI or on a server. The same snapshot
            runs in a container or natively, from a folder, a tarball or a deployed site's URL. Each named run is an
            <em>instance</em>.</p>

          <h2 class="h3" id="run-sessions">Sessions and instances</h2>
          <pre>snowglobe run dist/elixir                    <span class="dim"># a session; ctrl-] quits</span>
printf 'Enum.sum(1..100)\n' | snowglobe run dist/elixir
snowglobe run dist/elixir --name pg --detach
snowglobe exec pg 'sql "SELECT 1"'           <span class="dim"># prints only what the command printed</span>
snowglobe attach pg                          <span class="dim"># join it; ctrl-] detaches</span>
snowglobe ps
snowglobe stop pg</pre>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>In a container</strong> by default (<code>--runtime docker</code>): unprivileged, read-only,
              memory-capped.</li>
            <li><strong>Natively</strong> with <code>--runtime native</code> (or <code>SNOWGLOBE_RUNTIME=native</code>):
              it runs <code>snowglobe-vm</code> itself, with no Docker.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Piped input is typed in one line at a time, each waiting until the app goes quiet. Each instance starts
            fresh from the snapshot.</p>

          <h2 class="h3" id="remote">Sites from a URL</h2>
          <p>A site doesn't have to be local. <code>snowglobe run</code> also takes:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>a deployed site's URL, which it mirrors into a cache, or</li>
            <li>a tarball made with <code>snowglobe pack</code>, local or by URL.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Downloads happen on the host and are revalidated. The second run starts from the cache, and the sandbox
            stays offline.</p>
          <pre>snowglobe run <%= @site %>/python/
snowglobe pack dist/elixir -o elixir.tar.gz
snowglobe run https://cdn.example.com/elixir.tar.gz</pre>

          <h2 class="h3" id="ports">Serving from the guest</h2>
          <p>A site built with <code>--network fetch</code> can serve. <code>-p HOST:GUEST</code> forwards a port on
            your machine to one in the guest. Host ports bind to 127.0.0.1 unless you give an address:
            <code>-p 0.0.0.0:8080:4000</code>.</p>

          <h2 class="h3" id="bundle">One executable</h2>
          <p><code>snowglobe bundle</code> makes a site a single executable: <code>snowglobe-vm</code> with the site
            appended. It needs no Docker, hypervisor or KVM, so it runs on a laptop, in CI or inside someone else's
            container.</p>
          <p>Build with <code>--sandbox</code> for a bundle. The guest then keeps its file cache in the snapshot, and
            exercises run before it.</p>
          <pre>snowglobe build examples/python --sandbox --out dist/python
snowglobe bundle dist/python -o python        <span class="dim"># Linux; --target darwin for Apple silicon</span>
echo 'sum(i*i for i in range(10))' | ./python</pre>
          <p>A bundle whose app is a shell also runs one-shot jobs:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><code>--workspace DIR</code> is written into a fresh machine, <code>--cmd</code> runs there, and its
              output, exit status and <code>out/</code> come back.</li>
            <li><code>--serve SOCKET --jobs-root DIR</code> restores the snapshot once and answers each job with a
              fork of it, sharing the restored machine copy-on-write.</li>
            <li><code>--server SOCKET</code> is that server's client.</li>
          </ul>
          <pre>./toolkit --workspace job --cmd 'sh main.sh' --env 'N=42'
./toolkit --serve /run/toolkit.sock --jobs-root /var/lib/jobs &amp;
./toolkit --server /run/toolkit.sock --workspace /var/lib/jobs/1 --cmd 'sh main.sh'</pre>
          <h3 class="h4" id="job-limits">A job's limits</h3>
          <p>Past its <code>--timeout</code>, nothing a job may use is limited unless asked. Each limit is a flag of
            a one-shot job and the same field of a request to <code>--serve</code>, where every forked job has its
            own:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><code>--disk-limit MB</code> (<code>disk_limit_mb</code>): what the guest's file writes may hold
              (its filesystem is in the process's memory). Past it, writes fail with ENOSPC and <code>df</code>
              shows what is left. What the job prints has a limit of its own, outside it.</li>
            <li><code>--max-rss MB</code> (<code>max_rss_mb</code>): the process's (or the fork's) resident
              memory. Past it the job ends at once, out of memory (exit 137).</li>
            <li><code>--memory-limit MB</code>, <code>--cpus-limit N</code>, <code>--pids-limit N</code>
              (<code>memory_limit_mb</code>, <code>cpus_limit</code>, <code>pids_limit</code>): the command runs in
              a cgroup v2 in the guest, made at boot and reused, so a limit costs a job a few writes, not a
              process. A guest OOM kill ends it with 137, and whatever the job left running ends with it. The
              memory limit needs a site built with <code>snowglobe build --memory-limits</code> (its guest
              boots with the kernel's memory controller, which costs fork-heavy jobs about 5%); the CPU and
              process limits work on any site built by this snowglobe. A guest that can't enforce one (a site
              built without it, before its kernel had cgroups, or with no kernel) fails the job, saying
              so.</li>
            <li><code>--cpu-time S</code> (<code>cpu_time_s</code>): the process's CPU time, every thread. Past it
              the job ends (exit 152); RLIMIT_CPU backs it up.</li>
            <li><code>--output-limit BYTES</code> (<code>output_limit</code>): past it the command is killed and its
              output cut there (exit 153).</li>
            <li><code>--copy-limit MB</code> (<code>copy_limit_mb</code>, default 2048): the workspace copied in,
              and <code>out/</code> copied back, each. <code>out/</code> comes back even from a job out of time,
              and is never written through a symbolic link.</li>
            <li><code>--read-only-in</code> (<code>read_only_in</code>): <code>in/</code> is mounted read-only in
              the guest.</li>
            <li><code>--egress none|https|internet|private</code> (<code>egress</code>): <code>https</code> is TCP
              443 to public addresses only. A forked job's is at most its server's.</li>
          </ul>
          <p><code>--status-file PATH</code> writes the result as JSON as the run ends: <code>status</code> (null if
            the command didn't end by itself), <code>exit</code>, <code>timed_out</code>, <code>killed</code>,
            <code>oom</code>, <code>output_truncated</code>, <code>disk_full</code>, <code>cpu_time_exceeded</code>,
            <code>out_truncated</code>, <code>ms</code>. A server's answer carries the same fields. Each forked job
            is confined to its own workspace (Landlock, or Seatbelt), not the rest of the jobs root, can't
            connect to any Unix socket, and is cancelled when its client's connection closes. A job writes
            nothing its server shares with other jobs: a server's <code>--log-dir</code> has the server's own
            events (each job's log handles are closed before it runs), and the JIT profile a server starts
            from is only read, never added to by its jobs (a single-tenant <code>snowglobe run</code> or
            one-shot job still adds to it).</p>
          <pre>./toolkit --workspace job --cmd 'sh main.sh' --memory-limit 256 --disk-limit 512 --status-file job.json</pre>

          <h2 class="h3" id="shared-memory">Instances share their memory</h2>
          <p>Native instances of one site don't each hold a copy of its snapshot:</p>
          <ol>
            <li>The first one writes the snapshot's memory to an image in the cache.</li>
            <li>Every instance after it maps that image copy-on-write. Pages no instance writes are kept once for all
              of them. A page an instance writes becomes its own.</li>
            <li>An instance restores in a few milliseconds instead of decompressing the snapshot first.</li>
          </ol>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="compare">
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Ten instances of the Python run</th><th scope="col">Each, before</th><th scope="col">Each, now</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Memory on Linux (PSS)</th><td data-label="Before">111 MB</td><td data-label="Now">46 MB</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Memory on macOS (physical footprint)</th><td data-label="Before">175 MB</td><td data-label="Now">96 MB</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Restore</th><td data-label="Before">60–74 ms</td><td data-label="Now">4–6 ms</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Where:</strong> <code>ram/</code> under the cache (<code>SNOWGLOBE_CACHE</code>, by default
              <code>~/.cache/snowglobe</code>), one image per snapshot, named by its hash.</li>
            <li><strong>Disk:</strong> about as much as the snapshot's memory in use (71 MB for the Python run).
              Images unused for two weeks go when a new one is made.</li>
            <li><strong>Forks:</strong> machines forked from a template, by <code>--fork</code> or the daemon, already
              shared the template's memory and still do. Their template server maps the image too.</li>
            <li><strong>Off:</strong> <code>SNOWGLOBE_RAM_IMAGE=off</code> restores into memory of the instance's
              own.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>How the image is kept from changing under a machine is on <a href="../isolation/#security">Isolation</a>.</p>
          <p>Next: drive an instance from a program on <a href="../agents/">For agents and programs</a>, or run many
            machines on <a href="../orchestration/">Orchestration and fleets</a>.</p>
        </section>
